We'll use level and contour curves to construct the graph of the function

		f: R² -> R, 	f(x, y) = 4 - x² - y², 

By definition 1.4, the level curve at height c is 

		{(x, y) ∈ R² | 4 - x² - y² = c } = {(x, y) | x² + y² = 4 - c }.

Thus, we see that the level curves for c < 4 are circles centered at the origin of radius √(4 - c). 
The level "curve" at height c = 4 is not a curve at all but just a single point (the origin). 
Finally, there are no level curves at heights larger than 4 since the equation x² + y² = 4 - c has no real solutions in x and y. 
